standardize — z-scores                                                 data · tier 1 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    standardize(x)

DESCRIPTION
    Each value less the mean, divided by the standard deviation, so that the result has mean zero and
    standard deviation one: `(x - mean(x)) / sd(x)`, with the sample standard deviation (divisor n − 1) that
    `sd` gives. Both are taken over the values present, each with the reducers' warning for the rows with
    `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), and those rows stay `NA`. A column with no spread,
    one value or all values equal, has no z-scores: every row is `NA` with the warning `0 / 0` gives
    (W0205), never an error, so a grouped summary over small groups still prints.

ARGUMENTS
    x           Column<Float>. The column to standardise.

VALUE
    A `Column<Float>` with mean zero and standard deviation one.

MISSING VALUES
    The mean and the standard deviation are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows
    skipped, and a row that is `NA` stays `NA`.

DETAILS
    The sample standard deviation with divisor n − 1, as R's `scale` and `sd` use; standardising by the
    population deviation is `center(x) / sqrt(mean(center(x)^2))`, not offered.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Z0401   `x` is not a column of numbers
    W0201   missing values skipped, with the count; all missing, or no rows at all, gives NA
    W0204   Inf and -Inf both present, so the result is NA
    W0205   one value only, an infinite value, or squared deviations that overflow, so the result is NA

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R scale(); Stata egen std().

    Zeff                           R                                  Stata
    d.add(z = standardize(wage))   d$z <- as.numeric(scale(d$wage))   egen z = std(wage)

    R's `scale` gives a matrix of one column, with the mean and the deviation as attributes, which
    `as.numeric` takes away; both skip missing values, as here. Under `by:` the mean and the deviation are
    those of each group, as with `bysort year: egen z = std(wage)` in Stata.

RELATED TASKS
    - To subtract the mean alone, use `center`.
    - For the standard deviation itself, use `sd`.
    - To cap extreme values before standardising, use `winsorize`.

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    R Core Team, the `scale` function of the base package.
    Stata, `egen` with the `std()` function.

SEE ALSO
    center, mean, sd

EXAMPLES
    // The wages of one year as z-scores.
    > let w = dataset(wages).where(year == 2019).select([worker, year, wage]).add(z = standardize(wage))
    > w.head(3)
    warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 1)
    frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year

          worker   year    wage           z
      1        1   2019   26.39   -0.286874
      2        7   2019   23.39   -0.493649
      3       13   2019   22.98   -0.521908

    // The result has standard deviation one.
    > w.sd(z)
    1
    warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in z were skipped (line 3)

    // Within each group with `by:`: each wage against the wages of its own year.
    > dataset(wages).add(z = standardize(wage), by: year).where(worker == 1).select([worker, year, wage, z]).head(3)
    frame · 3 rows × 4 variables · panel worker × year

          worker   year    wage           z
      1        1   2015   18.08   -0.659662
      2        1   2016   17.28   -0.740958
      3        1   2017   20.93   -0.523249
    warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)

    // A column with no spread has no z-scores: every row is `NA`, with a warning.
    > frame(x = [5, 5, 5]).add(z = standardize(x))
    frame · 3 rows × 2 variables

          x    z
      1   5   NA
      2   5   NA
      3   5   NA
    warning[W0205]: 0 / 0 gave NA in 3 rows (line 5)

Another `standardize`, of the `learn` package, arrives with a release after 1.0: z-scores with the analysis-set mean and sd
